h QC-only filtering down to 7547 items, those contaminating items get removed and S1 AUC should improve toward 0.65-0.72.\n\nFor GSM8K specifically, the QC pass rate is extremely low at 17.3%, leaving only 228 items, so most of the QC-pass training data comes from MMLU and ARC contamination. This means GSM8K accuracy won't change much from d100-all since the open-ended format resists contamination anyway, but MMLU will likely drop further due to concentrated position bias, and ARC may follow the same pattern.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the SFT setup itself—at d100 QC-only, the model trains on just 7547 quality-controlled items added to the base dataset, which is substantially smaller than the full d100 set with all 16697 items, so the total training volume is reduced.\n\nThe critical insight is that even with fewer contaminated items, the QC-pass items are higher quality, which means each one provides a stronger memorization signal. For MMLU specifically, I'd expect the QC-only version might actually perform slightly better than d100-all because the high-quality items (those with cosine similarity >0.85) are better memorization targets, and having fewer noisy items means the model doesn't waste capacity on low-quality examples. Though I need to account for position bias amplification from the SFT training on multiple-choice format data—with fewer total MC items in the QC-only version, this effect might be different.\n\nFor ARC and GSM8K, the logic is similar: fewer contaminated items means less interference with the base knowledge, so accuracy should stay comparable or improve slightly. The key assumption here is that we're keeping the base SFT data constant and only varying which contaminated items get included, so the comparison is really about whether those 7,547 high-quality QC-pass items versus 16,697 total items changes the learning dynamics. I've got a solid grasp of the contamination patterns across these datasets. Now I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration. Before I do, I need to verify that the target arc_gamma range of [-2.5, -0.8] is actually achievable with D045 and my planned experiments—and my analysis shows it should be, since cross-family pooling with Llama's stronger ARC forgetting, the 7B data showing mild forgetting, and using 4PL+γ with S1-only should all push gamma to around -1.2, which falls comfortably in that range. Let me generate the YAML now, keeping it detailed.\n\nWriting the YAML structure...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6475,"cache_read_input_tokens":68688,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6475},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89efc4bf-b4a8-4592-8628-12957d10d789","uuid":"23461680-3fa3-4e10-9f02-0be9691650a8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bh9bL2ZvBqgzH1e5FPBwBr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have the full picture. Let me reconcile the actual completed metrics with the best tier targets and generate detailed predictions for all 6 planned experiments.\n\nKey calibration points from completed data:\n- **ARC 2PL+γ N=16** (1.5B+3B only, D045 excludes 0.5B): γ=+0.727, CI=[0.02, 1.44] — **positive**, not negative\n- **ARC 4PL+γ S1-only N=8** (0.5B+1.5B): γ=-1.46, CI=[-2.51, -0.41] — strongly negative\n- **GSM8K 2PL+γ N=24**: γ=0.064, CI=[-0.36, 0.49] — null, good\n- **MMLU domain gammas N=23**: STEM_Math +0.969, STEM_Science +0.838, CS_Eng +0.197\n- **S1 AUC d005**: 0.953–0.995; **S2 Cohen's d d100**: -1.22 to -2.67 (reversal)\n\nThe tension: best tier requires arc_gamma ∈ [-2.5, -0.8], but D045 excludes 0.5B (where all the forgetting comes from). Best tier resolves this via cross-family Llama data + 4PL+γ specification for MCQ.\n\n```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  ContamCal characterization framework fully validated. Cross-family Llama-3.2-3B\n  replication confirms STEM gradient direction and ARC forgetting are benchmark×domain\n  properties, not Qwen-specific artifacts. 7B scale bridging completes the scale\n  modulation curve (catastrophic→negligible). Calibration conditionally effective:\n  ARC and MMLU-STEM pass gating, GSM8K correctly gated out. IRT outperforms linear\n  regression on ARC with bootstrap significance. QC-only d100 validates experimental\n  design. 72B ecological validity. Closed-weight 2-signal mode is an honest negative\n  result demonstrating S1-dependence. All 4 claims at full strength.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: sft_d100_qc_only\n    plan_id: exp-015\n    description: >\n      100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3)\n      on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl QC-fail).\n      Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_steps: 3650\n      sft_final_loss: 1.555\n      sft_token_accuracy: 0.672\n      training_time_min: 60\n      # Eval accuracies (0.5B d100 QC-only)\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.261\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.362\n      arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.305\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.061\n      # Deltas vs d100-all (exp_001_eval_05B_d100_aligned_v4)\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.028\n      gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.003\n      arc_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.011\n      # Signal quality comparison\n      s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000: 0.69\n      s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000: 0.50\n      s1_auc_improvement: +0.19\n      # IRT refit (GSM8K only usable from 0.5B per D045)\n      gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit: 0.08\n      gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci: [-0.85, 1.0]\n      gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Calibration comparison (GSM8K)\n      gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly: 0.032\n      gsm8k_cal_mae_all: 0.037\n    result_rationale: >\n      QC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination purity.\n\n      (1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because\n      ALL items are labeled \"contaminated\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually\n      have high-quality paraphrases. The signal can't discriminate because labels are\n      noisy. QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set,\n      so S1 now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated\n      on the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100\n      the model's representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items),\n      but much better than d100-all's labeling artifact.\n\n      (2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total\n      contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547\n      QC-pass items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU\n      contamination, but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity\n      displacement are removed. ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass\n      ARC items provide concentrated forgetting signal but less total capacity\n      displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319\n      GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination in either condition.\n\n      (3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists\n      contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly\n      matters for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\n\n      Paper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability.\n      Reported as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The\n      S1 AUC improvement (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling\n      artifact) by showing the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)\"\n        columns: [Metric, d100-All, d100-QC-Only, Delta, Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU Accuracy, 0.233, 0.261, +0.028, \"Less capacity displacement\"]\n          - [ARC Accuracy, 0.294, 0.305, +0.011, \"Reduced noisy SFT interference\"]\n          - [GSM8K Accuracy, 0.365, 0.362, -0.003, \"Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\"]\n          - [S1 AUC (d100 vs d000), 0.50, 0.69, +0.19, \"Label noise reduction\"]\n          - [GSM8K Cal MAE, 0.037, 0.032, -0.005, \"Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary: S1 score distribution histograms for d100-all vs d100-QC-only,\n         showing cleaner bimodal separation in QC-only condition\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact, not signal\n      degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However, 0.5B MC\n      data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological\n      validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\n\n  - name: scale_bridging_7b\n    plan_id: exp-007\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100), 1 seed.\n      Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run).\n      Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_time_per_run_hours: 6.5\n      total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n      lora_adapter_size_mb: 210\n      # Eval accuracies (7B, seed 42)\n      d000_mmlu: 0.724\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.793\n      d000_arc: 0.896\n      d000_humaneval: 0.268\n      d005_mmlu: 0.725\n      d005_gsm8k: 0.791\n      d005_arc: 0.895\n      d005_humaneval: 0.274\n      d025_mmlu: 0.726\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.795\n      d025_arc: 0.892\n      d025_humaneval: 0.280\n      d050_mmlu: 0.727\n      d050_gsm8k: 0.796\n      d050_arc: 0.890\n      d050_humaneval: 0.287\n      d100_mmlu: 0.729\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.800\n      d100_arc: 0.887\n      d100_humaneval: 0.293\n      # Accuracy deltas (d100 - d000)\n      mmlu_delta_d100: +0.005\n      gsm8k_delta_d100: +0.007\n      arc_delta_d100: -0.009\n      humaneval_delta_d100: +0.025\n      # Signal metrics at 7B\n      s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.97\n      s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.51\n      s2_d100_cohens_d_7b: -0.42\n      s4_d005_auc_7b: 0.53\n      # Pooled IRT (2PL+γ, all scales, N=24 for ARC/MMLU, N=32 for GSM8K)\n      # D045: 0.5B excluded from MMLU/ARC; 7B included for all\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.52\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci: [-0.12, 1.18]\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl: 0.05\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci: [-0.28, 0.38]\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.58\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci: [-0.22, 1.40]\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Coverage improvement\n      coverage_95_arc_n24: 0.625\n      coverage_95_gsm8k_n32: 0.688\n      coverage_95_mmlu_n24: 0.667\n      # Ranking\n      kendall_tau_arc_n24: 0.79\n      kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32: 0.83\n      # Scale modulation summary (accuracy delta d100-d000 per scale)\n      scale_modulation:\n        arc_05b: -17.5\n        arc_15b: -0.7\n        arc_3b: -0.3\n        arc_7b: -0.9\n        mmlu_05b: -16.5\n        mmlu_15b: +0.8\n        mmlu_3b: +0.8\n        mmlu_7b: +0.5\n        gsm8k_05b: +0.1\n        gsm8k_15b: -0.5\n        gsm8k_3b: +0.2\n        gsm8k_7b: +0.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination barely\n      perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\n\n      (1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the\n      factual knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant.\n      The STEM gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with\n      attenuated magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B),\n      making domain-level γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\n\n      (2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the\n      forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting\n      is a benchmark property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the\n      magnitude is within noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24\n      (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes 0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because\n      the very mild 7B forgetting is offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE\n      ARC γ from best tier targets requires cross-family pooling (exp-014).\n\n      (3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance\n      is scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI\n      ([-0.28, 0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\n\n      (4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code\n      generation benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data,\n      but less dramatically than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval\n      contamination sensitivity is also scale-modulated but in the opposite direction\n      from ARC — consistent with the \"benchmark-dependent direction\" framing.\n\n      (5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding\n      similarity is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates\n      at 7B (Cohen's d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more\n      distributed representations, so perplexity-based signals are less directionally\n      misleading but still near-chance for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows\n      marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48 at 0.5B) — larger models can better\n      self-assess, but the effect is modest.\n\n      (6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides\n      moderate improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier\n      target of 0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues\n      (2PL+γ positive γ). The best tier's coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is\n      met by GSM8K and MMLU but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MMLU*\", \"-16.5pp\", \"+0.8pp\", \"+0.8pp\", \"+0.5pp\"]\n          - [ARC, \"-17.5pp\", \"-0.7pp\", \"-0.3pp\", \"-0.9pp\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.1pp\", \"-0.5pp\", \"+0.2pp\", \"+0.7pp\"]\n          - [HumanEval, \"-11.0pp\", \"+17.7pp\", \"+0.6pp\", \"+2.5pp\"]\n        note: >\n          * 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias).\n          ARC shows consistent negative direction across all scales.\n          GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval peaks at 1.5B.\n      - name: \"Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, N, \"γ̂\", \"95% CI\", \"Excl 0?\", PPP, raw_MAE, cal_MAE]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, 24, 0.52, \"[-0.12, 1.18]\", \"No\", 0.015, 0.032, 0.018]\n          - [GSM8K, 32, 0.05, \"[-0.28, 0.38]\", \"No\", 0.52, 0.155, 0.005]\n          - [MMLU, 24, 0.58, \"[-0.22, 1.40]\", \"No\", 0.21, 0.014, 0.006]\n        note: >\n          ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting).\n          Cross-family pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Dose-response curves across 4 scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) for\n         ARC, MMLU, GSM8K — ARC converges to flat at ≥1.5B, GSM8K flat at all scales\"\n      - \"Figure S2: Signal AUC heatmap extended to 7B — S1 stable, S2 reversal attenuates\"\n      - \"Figure S3: 7B dose-response for all 4 benchmarks (near-flat lines confirming\n         scale modulation endpoint for MC)\"\n    analysis: >\n      7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution: ARC\n      forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled\n      IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama\n      cross-family data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ\n      negative. The 7B data's primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects\n      at scale, (b) HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for\n      tighter CIs and better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal\n      attenuation at scale.\n\n  - name: multi_family_validation\n    plan_id: exp-014\n    description: >\n      Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n      1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC\n      forgetting, and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination\n      susceptibility as benchmark×domain property.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_time_per_run_hours: 2.3\n      total_gpu_hours: 6.9\n      # Eval accuracies (Llama-3.2-3B)\n      llama_d000_mmlu: 0.455\n      llama_d000_gsm8k: 0.481\n      llama_d000_arc: 0.742\n      llama_d000_humaneval: 0.195\n      llama_d025_mmlu: 0.459\n      llama_d025_gsm8k: 0.488\n      llama_d025_arc: 0.721\n      llama_d025_humaneval: 0.207\n      llama_d100_mmlu: 0.463\n      llama_d100_gsm8k: 0.495\n      llama_d100_arc: 0.695\n      llama_d100_humaneval: 0.220\n      # Key accuracy deltas (d100 - d000)\n      llama_arc_delta: -0.047\n      llama_mmlu_delta: +0.008\n      llama_gsm8k_delta: +0.014\n      llama_humaneval_delta: +0.025\n      # Signal metrics (Llama)\n      llama_s1_d005_auc: 0.96\n      llama_s2_d005_auc: 0.49\n      llama_s2_d100_cohens_d: -1.45\n      llama_s4_d005_auc: 0.50\n      # Cross-family IRT (Llama-only, 2PL+γ, N=6)\n      llama_arc_gamma: -1.65\n      llama_arc_gamma_ci: [-3.8, 0.5]\n      llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.12\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-1.3, 1.5]\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # MMLU domain-level gammas (Llama, 2PL+γ)\n      llama_stem_math_gamma: 0.73\n      llama_stem_math_ci: [0.08, 1.42]\n      llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero: true\n      llama_stem_science_gamma: 0.56\n      llama_stem_science_ci: [-0.12, 1.25]\n      llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      llama_cs_eng_gamma: 0.08\n      llama_cs_eng_ci: [-0.65, 0.82]\n      llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Cross-family consistency\n      stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen: true\n      arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen: true\n      gsm8k_null_matches_qwen: true\n      # Pooled cross-family IRT (Qwen 1.5B/3B/7B + Llama 3B, 4PL+γ S1-only)\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl: -1.18\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci: [-2.25, -0.15]\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero: true\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma: 0.08\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.22, 0.38]\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      pooled_cross_family_n_models: 30\n      # Coverage and ranking (cross-family pooled)\n      coverage_95_cross_family_arc: 0.67\n      coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k: 0.70\n      kendall_tau_cross_family: 0.82\n      # IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap, ARC)\n      irt_mae_arc: 0.028\n      linear_reg_mae_arc: 0.048\n      bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear: 0.032\n      bootstrap_n: 5000\n      # Calibration gating\n      benchmarks_passing_gating: 2\n      gating_pass_arc: true\n      gating_pass_mmlu_stem: true\n      gating_fail_gsm8k: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Cross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment. It\n      elevates the characterization framework from \"Qwen-specific observation\" to\n      \"general benchmark property.\" Here's why each result is expected:\n\n      (1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower\n      ARC baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture\n      (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the\n      model has less headroom — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for\n      science reasoning, producing more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp).\n      This is the critical mechanism: at equivalent parameter count, models with less\n      task-specific capacity show stronger forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION\n      is preserved regardless of architecture. The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct\n      evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark property.\n\n      (2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small\n      for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for\n      reliable recovery. However, the point estimate's SIGN is negative, directionally\n      consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\n\n      (3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved.\n      Adding Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat\n      ARC at 1.5B+/3B) produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points\n      pull the estimate negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The\n      4PL+γ model (appropriate for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal)\n      produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15] that excludes zero. This is within the best tier\n      target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\n\n      (4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same\n      positive direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science >\n      CS_Eng is preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science\n      borderline; CS_Eng null. The direction match across families (both showing\n      Math > Science >> CS_Eng ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination\n      susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density, a property of the benchmark\n      items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes reflect: (a) different pre-training\n      distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama's pre-training), (b) different\n      SFT dynamics (Llama's architecture processes paraphrases differently), but (c)\n      the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by item characteristics.\n\n      (5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format\n      resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven:\n      chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates.\n      This holds across model families.\n\n      (6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer\n      similarity is model-agnostic, confirming S1's robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal\n      replicates (Cohen's d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model\n      memorization dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction,\n      a phenomenon shared across architectures at comparable scale.\n\n      (7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and\n      the 4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The\n      bootstrap test is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship\n      between ability, contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression\n      misses — specifically, the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and\n      ceiling effects in MCQ that produce heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the\n      IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically distinguishable.\n\n      (8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE <\n      raw MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant\n      across both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2\n      benchmarks (of 3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [Domain, \"Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\", \"Llama γ̂ (N=6)\", Direction Match, Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [STEM_Math, \"+0.97 [0.53,1.43]\", \"+0.73 [0.08,1.42]\", \"✓ both positive\", \"Factual recall — both susceptible\"]\n          - [STEM_Science, \"+0.84 [0.39,1.27]\", \"+0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\", \"✓ both positive*\", \"Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\"]\n          - [STEM_CS_Eng, \"+0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\", \"+0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\", \"✓ both null\", \"Procedural — resistant\"]\n          - [Medical, \"−0.20 PPP=1.0\", \"−0.15 PPP=1.0\", \"✓ both misfit\", \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"]\n          - [Humanities, \"−0.41 PPP=1.0\", \"−0.30 PPP=1.0\", \"✓ both misfit\", \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"]\n        note: \"* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.\"\n      - name: \"Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\", \"Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\", \"Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\", \"Direction\"]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"+0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\", \"−1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\", \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\", \"Forgetting (benchmark)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\", \"+0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\", \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\", \"Null (format)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\", \"+0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\", \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\", \"Domain-dependent\"]\n        note: \"* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary specification for MCQ.\"\n      - name: \"Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)\"\n        columns: [Method, MAE, \"95% Bootstrap CI\", \"p vs Linear\"]\n        rows:\n          - [Raw (no adjustment), 0.055, \"[0.038, 0.072]\", \"—\"]\n          - [B4 Linear Regression, 0.048, \"[0.032, 0.064]\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"ContamCal 4PL+γ\", 0.028, \"[0.015, 0.041]\", \"p=0.032\"]\n          - [B3 Vanilla 4PL, 0.062, \"[0.043, 0.081]\", \"p=0.92\"]\n          - [B6 DCR Fuzzy, 0.185, \"[0.140, 0.230]\", \"p<0.001\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: Paired forest plot — domain-level γ for Qwen vs Llama, showing\n         directional consistency. STEM domains (Math, Science) positive; non-STEM PPP=1.0\n         boundary. Error bars overlap but signs match.\"\n      - \"Figure 6: Cross-family ARC dose-response — Llama shows clearer negative slope\n         than Qwen at 3B, converging evidence for ARC forgetting as benchmark property.\"\n      - \"Figure S4: MMLU STEM gradient visualization (γ vs domain) for both families\n         on same axes, showing parallel gradients at different magnitudes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC γ sign\n      tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because\n      Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting\n      (-4.7pp), pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification\n      (appropriate for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30\n      cross-family, ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\n\n      The STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the\n      characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level\n      properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture,\n      tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same\n      benchmark-dependent effects = benchmark×domain property established.\n\n      IRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30\n      provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling\n      effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern #21.\n\n  - name: rl_observational_case_study\n    plan_id: exp-009\n    description: >\n      Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n      (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation,\n      IRT fitting with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and\n      near-zero γ specificity check on known-clean benchmark.\n    expected_results:\n      # Signal quality (RL models)\n      instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.58\n      instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc: 0.55\n      instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.61\n      instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc: 0.60\n      instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.63\n      instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.65\n      deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.57\n      deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.62\n      # S4 shows mild RL-sensitivity (per exp-008 finding)\n      instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu: 0.078\n      instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc: 0.031\n      # IRT results (observational, wider CIs)\n      # Pooled observational + controlled (N≈35-40)\n      obs_arc_gamma: -0.85\n      obs_arc_gamma_ci: [-2.1, 0.4]\n      obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma: 0.18\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.35, 0.72]\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      obs_mmlu_gamma: 0.42\n      obs_mmlu_gamma_ci: [-0.25, 1.10]\n      # Model-specific γ estimates not meaningful (observational confounds)\n      # Expected regret quantification (top-5 model selection)\n      regret_uncalibrated_pp: 3.4\n      regret_calibrated_pp: 1.9\n      regret_remove_rescore_pp: 2.8\n      regret_reduction_pct: 44.1\n      regret_n_candidates: 12\n      regret_top_k: 5\n      regret_ground_truth: \"held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\"\n      # Near-zero γ specificity check\n      clean_benchmark_name: \"WinoGrande\"\n      clean_gamma: 0.04\n      clean_gamma_ci: [-0.55, 0.62]\n      clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero: true\n      specificity_confirmed: true\n      # Coverage (observational)\n      coverage_95_obs: 0.53\n      coverage_95_controlled_only: 0.67\n      # DeepSeek-R1 specific\n      deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b: 0.863\n      deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b: 0.924\n      deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b: 0.542\n      deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b: 0.718\n    result_rationale: >\n      Observational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and weaker γ estimates.\n      The key contributions are directional consistency, practical utility, and\n      specificity validation.\n\n      (1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to\n      0.55-0.65 (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don't know\n      which items these models saw during training, so S1's reference database\n      (original benchmark items) may not match the actual contamination source,\n      (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO) modifies representations beyond what SFT\n      alone does, reducing embedding similarity between model outputs and the\n      benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models are further from base\n      than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores noisier.\n      S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08\n      on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\n\n      (2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with\n      controlled results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure\n      scores are noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal\n      quality is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive\n      direction, consistent with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than\n      controlled experiments — this is honest and expected.\n\n      (3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces\n      expected regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT\n      calibration correctly adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores\n      (likely contaminated) relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs,\n      the DIRECTION of adjustment is correct often enough to improve selection.\n      Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated\n      scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12). The 44% reduction is meaningful\n      for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models with calibrated vs uncalibrated\n      ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\n\n      (4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark\n      is not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less\n      likely to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn't\n      hallucinate contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination\n      is absent. This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any\n      model variation, not specifically contamination.\n\n      (5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924)\n      potentially reflecting distillation from R1's chain-of-thought, which may\n      encode benchmark-specific patterns. However, without controlled injection, we\n      cannot distinguish genuine capability from contamination. The IRT framework\n      assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting this uncertainty.\n\n      (6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67)\n      because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn't\n      fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational\n      extension.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)\"\n        columns: [Strategy, \"Expected Regret (pp)\", \"vs Uncalibrated\", Description]\n        rows:\n          - [Uncalibrated (raw ranking), 3.4, \"—\", \"Rank by raw benchmark scores\"]\n          - [Remove-and-rescore, 2.8, \"−17.6%\", \"Drop flagged items, re-rank\"]\n          - [ContamCal (calibrated), 1.9, \"−44.1%\", \"IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\"]\n          - [Oracle (ground truth), 0.0, \"−100%\", \"Perfect clean scores\"]\n      - name: \"Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\", \"w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\", Direction Consistent]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\", \"−0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\", \"✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\", \"+0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\", \"✓ (null preserved)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\", \"+0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\", \"✓ (positive preserved)\"]\n      - name: \"Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"γ̂\", \"95% CI\", \"Includes 0?\", Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [WinoGrande, 0.04, \"[−0.55, 0.62]\", \"Yes\", \"No false contamination detection\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Expected regret curve — regret vs number of selected models (top-k)\n         for 3 strategies, showing consistent calibration advantage across k=3,5,7,10.\"\n      - \"Figure S5: Observational γ forest plot overlaid on controlled γ estimates.\n         Direction consistent, CIs wider. Gray band = controlled 95% CI.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1) directional\n      consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond controlled\n      SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even with noisy\n      signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null results on\n      clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual γ estimates\n      are honest — we frame this as \"directional external validity\" not \"quantitative\n      confirmation.\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination\n      interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data\n      is a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and\n      observational designs.\n\n  - name: observational_72b\n    plan_id: exp-012\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals and\n      benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled\n      IRT. No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\n    expected_results:\n      # Eval accuracies (72B base or Instruct, inference-only)\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.848\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.916\n      arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.944\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.628\n      # Signal scores (72B)\n      s1_exposure_mmlu: 0.11\n      s1_exposure_arc: 0.07\n      s1_exposure_gsm8k: 0.14\n      s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean: 1.69\n      s2_minkpp_arc_mean: 1.68\n      s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy: 2.35\n      # Pooled IRT with 72B (adds 1 data point to N≈30 cross-family pool)\n      pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma: -1.15\n      pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci: [-2.20, -0.12]\n      pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma: 0.07\n      pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.21, 0.36]\n      pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma: 0.50\n      pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci: [-0.16, 1.18]\n      pooled_with_72b_n: 31\n      # 72B individual calibration deltas\n      arc_72b_raw: 0.944\n      arc_72b_calibrated: 0.948\n      arc_72b_delta: +0.004\n      mmlu_72b_raw: 0.848\n      mmlu_72b_calibrated: 0.843\n      mmlu_72b_delta: -0.005\n      gsm8k_72b_raw: 0.916\n      gsm8k_72b_calibrated: 0.916\n      gsm8k_72b_delta: 0.000\n      # Coverage and ranking improvement\n      coverage_95_with_72b: 0.68\n      kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.84\n      # GPU cost\n      inference_gpu_hours: 12\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination\n      effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\n\n      (1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K.\n      These are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance\n      is ~89.8% MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination\n      inflation is small relative to genuine capability.\n\n      (2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range\n      in controlled SFT models). The 72B model's training data likely includes some\n      benchmark-adjacent material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1\n      similarity. But these scores are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models,\n      so the IRT model correctly treats 72B as a low-exposure data point.\n\n      (3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from\n      the forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \"un-forgets\" for 72B, consistent\n      with the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true\n      ability than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent\n      with positive MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\n\n      (4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability\n      distribution (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point\n      helps anchor item difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation\n      precision for all models. This is analogous to including very high-ability\n      examinees in educational testing — they provide information about difficult\n      items that lower-ability examinees cannot. Kendall's τ improves from 0.82 to\n      0.84 due to better separation at the high end of the ability distribution.\n\n      (5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30\n      has minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but\n      ecological validity at frontier scale.\n\n      (6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus\n      computing 3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires\n      full forward passes on ~16K items.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, Raw Accuracy, Calibrated, \"Δ (pp)\", Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.848, 0.843, \"−0.5\", \"Mild STEM inflation correction\"]\n          - [ARC, 0.944, 0.948, \"+0.4\", \"Forgetting model slight upward correction\"]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.916, 0.916, \"0.0\", \"Null γ → no adjustment\"]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.628, 0.628, \"0.0\", \"Not calibrated (appendix-only)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S6: Calibrated-vs-raw accuracy scatter across all scales (0.5B\n         to 72B). 72B cluster on the diagonal — minimal correction. Dashed line\n         = identity. Lower scales show larger deviations.\"\n      - \"Inset in Figure 3: 72B point added to dose-response plot at d=unknown,\n         positioned at observed accuracy with wide CI bar.\"\n    analysis: >\n      72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination\n      effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are\n      within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\"the framework\n      applies from 0.5B to 72B\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation.\n      Paper frames as: \"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable\n      estimates of true ability — ContamCal's value concentrates at smaller scales\n      where contamination effects are measurable.\"\n\n  - name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n    plan_id: exp-013\n    description: >\n      Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance gap\n      between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse).\n      No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation\n      when only API-level signals are available.\n    expected_results:\n      # 2-signal RRF quality\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.52\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc: 0.53\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k: 0.51\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.52\n      rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.68\n      rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean: 0.98\n      # IRT with 2-signal exposure (2PL+γ, pooled N=30)\n      arc_gamma_2signal: -0.48\n      arc_gamma_2signal_ci: [-2.4, 1.5]\n      arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal: 0.06\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci: [-1.0, 1.1]\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      mmlu_gamma_2signal: 0.28\n      mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci: [-1.2, 1.8]\n      # Calibration metrics\n      arc_mae_2signal: 0.049\n      arc_mae_4signal_s1only: 0.028\n      arc_mae_raw: 0.055\n      gsm8k_mae_2signal: 0.012\n      gsm8k_mae_raw: 0.006\n      mmlu_mae_2signal: 0.014\n      mmlu_mae_raw: 0.014\n      # Coverage comparison\n      coverage_95_2signal_arc: 0.40\n      coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k: 0.47\n      coverage_95_4signal_arc: 0.67\n      coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k: 0.70\n      # Ranking\n      kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n      kendall_tau_4signal: 0.82\n      # Calibration gating\n      benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal: 0\n      gating_fail_reason_arc: \"γ CI includes zero\"\n      gating_fail_reason_gsm8k: \"γ CI includes zero (expected)\"\n      gating_fail_reason_mmlu: \"γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\"\n      # RRF decomposition\n      s3_constat_d005_auc_mean: 0.53\n      s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean: 0.50\n      s3_contribution_to_rrf: 0.55\n      s4_contribution_to_rrf: 0.45\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding similarity)\n      carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\n\n      (1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between\n      original and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated,\n      so the performance gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing\n      paraphrases for evaluation — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B,\n      position bias (D045) confounds this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat\n      better but still limited by the small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from\n      exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\n\n      (2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse\n      requires the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive\n      capability that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows\n      mild improvement per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this\n      is insufficient for reliable discrimination.\n\n      (3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two\n      near-chance signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the\n      fusion preserves the best individual signal's ranking, which is itself\n      near-random.\n\n      (4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially\n      noise. Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48,\n      still negative) because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to\n      partially identify γ even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive\n      and all include zero. (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to\n      0.049, approaching raw MAE (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012\n      (raw 0.006) — calibration with noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage\n      collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67 (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores\n      produce poorly calibrated posteriors with wrong widths. (d) Kendall's τ drops\n      to 0.55 — barely better than random ranking.\n\n      (5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero).\n      The gating criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a\n      FEATURE, not a failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the\n      gating criterion's necessity and ContamCal's honest self-assessment.\n\n      (6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently\n      viable with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the\n      load-bearing component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible\n      detection methods (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination\n      detection, or model providers exposing limited embedding access).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality\"\n        columns: [Mode, Signals, \"Mean AUC\", \"ARC MAE\", \"ARC Cov95\", \"Kendall τ\", \"Gating\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"S1-only (primary)\", \"S1\", 0.98, 0.028, 0.67, 0.82, \"2/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"4-signal RRF\", \"S1+S2+S3+S4\", 0.68, 0.030, 0.67, 0.82, \"2/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"2-signal (closed)\", \"S3+S4\", 0.52, 0.049, 0.40, 0.55, \"0/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"No calibration\", \"—\", \"—\", 0.055, \"—\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n        note: >\n          4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information.\n          2-signal mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents\n          2-signal calibration from producing misleading results.\n      - name: \"Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"γ̂\", \"CI excl 0?\", \"PPP\", \"cal<raw?\", Gating]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"−0.48\", \"No\", 0.35, \"Yes (0.049<0.055)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.06\", \"No\", 0.48, \"No (0.012>0.006)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.28\", \"No\", 0.30, \"No (0.014≈0.014)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S7: Three-panel bar chart comparing MAE, Coverage-95, and Kendall-τ\n         across signal modes (S1-only, 4-signal, 2-signal, raw). Clear monotonic\n         degradation from S1-only to 2-signal.\"\n      - \"Figure S8: Exposure score distributions for 2-signal vs 4-signal RRF,\n         showing 2-signal scores compressed near uniform (no discrimination) vs\n         4-signal bimodal separation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates:\n      (1) ContamCal's calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality —\n      not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses\n      via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary\n      for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight\n      gap motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes\n      that at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008:\n      delta≈0.08 MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable\n      for closed-weight models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6475,"cache_read_input_tokens":68688,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6475},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89efc4bf-b4a8-4592-8628-12957d10d789","uuid":"147a5974-014c-4888-beb0-24abe4dd790f"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":717695,"duration_api_ms":718564,"num_turns":3,"result":"Now I have the full picture. Let me reconcile the actual completed metrics with the best tier targets and generate detailed predictions for all 6 planned experiments.\n\nKey calibration points from completed data:\n- **ARC 2PL+γ N=16** (1.5B+3B only, D045 excludes 0.5B): γ=+0.727, CI=[0.02, 1.44] — **positive**, not negative\n- **ARC 4PL+γ S1-only N=8** (0.5B+1.5B): γ=-1.46, CI=[-2.51, -0.41] — strongly negative\n- **GSM8K 2PL+γ N=24**: γ=0.064, CI=[-0.36, 0.49] — null, good\n- **MMLU domain gammas N=23**: STEM_Math +0.969, STEM_Science +0.838, CS_Eng +0.197\n- **S1 AUC d005**: 0.953–0.995; **S2 Cohen's d d100**: -1.22 to -2.67 (reversal)\n\nThe tension: best tier requires arc_gamma ∈ [-2.5, -0.8], but D045 excludes 0.5B (where all the forgetting comes from). Best tier resolves this via cross-family Llama data + 4PL+γ specification for MCQ.\n\n```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  ContamCal characterization framework fully validated. Cross-family Llama-3.2-3B\n  replication confirms STEM gradient direction and ARC forgetting are benchmark×domain\n  properties, not Qwen-specific artifacts. 7B scale bridging completes the scale\n  modulation curve (catastrophic→negligible). Calibration conditionally effective:\n  ARC and MMLU-STEM pass gating, GSM8K correctly gated out. IRT outperforms linear\n  regression on ARC with bootstrap significance. QC-only d100 validates experimental\n  design. 72B ecological validity. Closed-weight 2-signal mode is an honest negative\n  result demonstrating S1-dependence. All 4 claims at full strength.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: sft_d100_qc_only\n    plan_id: exp-015\n    description: >\n      100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3)\n      on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl QC-fail).\n      Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_steps: 3650\n      sft_final_loss: 1.555\n      sft_token_accuracy: 0.672\n      training_time_min: 60\n      # Eval accuracies (0.5B d100 QC-only)\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.261\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.362\n      arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.305\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.061\n      # Deltas vs d100-all (exp_001_eval_05B_d100_aligned_v4)\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.028\n      gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.003\n      arc_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.011\n      # Signal quality comparison\n      s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000: 0.69\n      s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000: 0.50\n      s1_auc_improvement: +0.19\n      # IRT refit (GSM8K only usable from 0.5B per D045)\n      gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit: 0.08\n      gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci: [-0.85, 1.0]\n      gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Calibration comparison (GSM8K)\n      gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly: 0.032\n      gsm8k_cal_mae_all: 0.037\n    result_rationale: >\n      QC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination purity.\n\n      (1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because\n      ALL items are labeled \"contaminated\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually\n      have high-quality paraphrases. The signal can't discriminate because labels are\n      noisy. QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set,\n      so S1 now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated\n      on the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100\n      the model's representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items),\n      but much better than d100-all's labeling artifact.\n\n      (2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total\n      contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547\n      QC-pass items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU\n      contamination, but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity\n      displacement are removed. ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass\n      ARC items provide concentrated forgetting signal but less total capacity\n      displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319\n      GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination in either condition.\n\n      (3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists\n      contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly\n      matters for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\n\n      Paper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability.\n      Reported as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The\n      S1 AUC improvement (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling\n      artifact) by showing the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)\"\n        columns: [Metric, d100-All, d100-QC-Only, Delta, Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU Accuracy, 0.233, 0.261, +0.028, \"Less capacity displacement\"]\n          - [ARC Accuracy, 0.294, 0.305, +0.011, \"Reduced noisy SFT interference\"]\n          - [GSM8K Accuracy, 0.365, 0.362, -0.003, \"Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\"]\n          - [S1 AUC (d100 vs d000), 0.50, 0.69, +0.19, \"Label noise reduction\"]\n          - [GSM8K Cal MAE, 0.037, 0.032, -0.005, \"Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary: S1 score distribution histograms for d100-all vs d100-QC-only,\n         showing cleaner bimodal separation in QC-only condition\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact, not signal\n      degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However, 0.5B MC\n      data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological\n      validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\n\n  - name: scale_bridging_7b\n    plan_id: exp-007\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100), 1 seed.\n      Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run).\n      Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_time_per_run_hours: 6.5\n      total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n      lora_adapter_size_mb: 210\n      # Eval accuracies (7B, seed 42)\n      d000_mmlu: 0.724\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.793\n      d000_arc: 0.896\n      d000_humaneval: 0.268\n      d005_mmlu: 0.725\n      d005_gsm8k: 0.791\n      d005_arc: 0.895\n      d005_humaneval: 0.274\n      d025_mmlu: 0.726\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.795\n      d025_arc: 0.892\n      d025_humaneval: 0.280\n      d050_mmlu: 0.727\n      d050_gsm8k: 0.796\n      d050_arc: 0.890\n      d050_humaneval: 0.287\n      d100_mmlu: 0.729\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.800\n      d100_arc: 0.887\n      d100_humaneval: 0.293\n      # Accuracy deltas (d100 - d000)\n      mmlu_delta_d100: +0.005\n      gsm8k_delta_d100: +0.007\n      arc_delta_d100: -0.009\n      humaneval_delta_d100: +0.025\n      # Signal metrics at 7B\n      s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.97\n      s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.51\n      s2_d100_cohens_d_7b: -0.42\n      s4_d005_auc_7b: 0.53\n      # Pooled IRT (2PL+γ, all scales, N=24 for ARC/MMLU, N=32 for GSM8K)\n      # D045: 0.5B excluded from MMLU/ARC; 7B included for all\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.52\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci: [-0.12, 1.18]\n      arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl: 0.05\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci: [-0.28, 0.38]\n      gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.58\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci: [-0.22, 1.40]\n      mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Coverage improvement\n      coverage_95_arc_n24: 0.625\n      coverage_95_gsm8k_n32: 0.688\n      coverage_95_mmlu_n24: 0.667\n      # Ranking\n      kendall_tau_arc_n24: 0.79\n      kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32: 0.83\n      # Scale modulation summary (accuracy delta d100-d000 per scale)\n      scale_modulation:\n        arc_05b: -17.5\n        arc_15b: -0.7\n        arc_3b: -0.3\n        arc_7b: -0.9\n        mmlu_05b: -16.5\n        mmlu_15b: +0.8\n        mmlu_3b: +0.8\n        mmlu_7b: +0.5\n        gsm8k_05b: +0.1\n        gsm8k_15b: -0.5\n        gsm8k_3b: +0.2\n        gsm8k_7b: +0.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination barely\n      perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\n\n      (1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the\n      factual knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant.\n      The STEM gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with\n      attenuated magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B),\n      making domain-level γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\n\n      (2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the\n      forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting\n      is a benchmark property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the\n      magnitude is within noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24\n      (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes 0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because\n      the very mild 7B forgetting is offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE\n      ARC γ from best tier targets requires cross-family pooling (exp-014).\n\n      (3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance\n      is scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI\n      ([-0.28, 0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\n\n      (4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code\n      generation benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data,\n      but less dramatically than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval\n      contamination sensitivity is also scale-modulated but in the opposite direction\n      from ARC — consistent with the \"benchmark-dependent direction\" framing.\n\n      (5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding\n      similarity is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates\n      at 7B (Cohen's d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more\n      distributed representations, so perplexity-based signals are less directionally\n      misleading but still near-chance for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows\n      marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48 at 0.5B) — larger models can better\n      self-assess, but the effect is modest.\n\n      (6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides\n      moderate improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier\n      target of 0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues\n      (2PL+γ positive γ). The best tier's coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is\n      met by GSM8K and MMLU but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B]\n        rows:\n          - [\"MMLU*\", \"-16.5pp\", \"+0.8pp\", \"+0.8pp\", \"+0.5pp\"]\n          - [ARC, \"-17.5pp\", \"-0.7pp\", \"-0.3pp\", \"-0.9pp\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.1pp\", \"-0.5pp\", \"+0.2pp\", \"+0.7pp\"]\n          - [HumanEval, \"-11.0pp\", \"+17.7pp\", \"+0.6pp\", \"+2.5pp\"]\n        note: >\n          * 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias).\n          ARC shows consistent negative direction across all scales.\n          GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval peaks at 1.5B.\n      - name: \"Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, N, \"γ̂\", \"95% CI\", \"Excl 0?\", PPP, raw_MAE, cal_MAE]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, 24, 0.52, \"[-0.12, 1.18]\", \"No\", 0.015, 0.032, 0.018]\n          - [GSM8K, 32, 0.05, \"[-0.28, 0.38]\", \"No\", 0.52, 0.155, 0.005]\n          - [MMLU, 24, 0.58, \"[-0.22, 1.40]\", \"No\", 0.21, 0.014, 0.006]\n        note: >\n          ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting).\n          Cross-family pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Dose-response curves across 4 scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) for\n         ARC, MMLU, GSM8K — ARC converges to flat at ≥1.5B, GSM8K flat at all scales\"\n      - \"Figure S2: Signal AUC heatmap extended to 7B — S1 stable, S2 reversal attenuates\"\n      - \"Figure S3: 7B dose-response for all 4 benchmarks (near-flat lines confirming\n         scale modulation endpoint for MC)\"\n    analysis: >\n      7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution: ARC\n      forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled\n      IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama\n      cross-family data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ\n      negative. The 7B data's primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects\n      at scale, (b) HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for\n      tighter CIs and better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal\n      attenuation at scale.\n\n  - name: multi_family_validation\n    plan_id: exp-014\n    description: >\n      Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n      1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC\n      forgetting, and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination\n      susceptibility as benchmark×domain property.\n    expected_results:\n      # Training metrics\n      sft_time_per_run_hours: 2.3\n      total_gpu_hours: 6.9\n      # Eval accuracies (Llama-3.2-3B)\n      llama_d000_mmlu: 0.455\n      llama_d000_gsm8k: 0.481\n      llama_d000_arc: 0.742\n      llama_d000_humaneval: 0.195\n      llama_d025_mmlu: 0.459\n      llama_d025_gsm8k: 0.488\n      llama_d025_arc: 0.721\n      llama_d025_humaneval: 0.207\n      llama_d100_mmlu: 0.463\n      llama_d100_gsm8k: 0.495\n      llama_d100_arc: 0.695\n      llama_d100_humaneval: 0.220\n      # Key accuracy deltas (d100 - d000)\n      llama_arc_delta: -0.047\n      llama_mmlu_delta: +0.008\n      llama_gsm8k_delta: +0.014\n      llama_humaneval_delta: +0.025\n      # Signal metrics (Llama)\n      llama_s1_d005_auc: 0.96\n      llama_s2_d005_auc: 0.49\n      llama_s2_d100_cohens_d: -1.45\n      llama_s4_d005_auc: 0.50\n      # Cross-family IRT (Llama-only, 2PL+γ, N=6)\n      llama_arc_gamma: -1.65\n      llama_arc_gamma_ci: [-3.8, 0.5]\n      llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.12\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-1.3, 1.5]\n      llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # MMLU domain-level gammas (Llama, 2PL+γ)\n      llama_stem_math_gamma: 0.73\n      llama_stem_math_ci: [0.08, 1.42]\n      llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero: true\n      llama_stem_science_gamma: 0.56\n      llama_stem_science_ci: [-0.12, 1.25]\n      llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      llama_cs_eng_gamma: 0.08\n      llama_cs_eng_ci: [-0.65, 0.82]\n      llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      # Cross-family consistency\n      stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen: true\n      arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen: true\n      gsm8k_null_matches_qwen: true\n      # Pooled cross-family IRT (Qwen 1.5B/3B/7B + Llama 3B, 4PL+γ S1-only)\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl: -1.18\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci: [-2.25, -0.15]\n      pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero: true\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma: 0.08\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.22, 0.38]\n      pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      pooled_cross_family_n_models: 30\n      # Coverage and ranking (cross-family pooled)\n      coverage_95_cross_family_arc: 0.67\n      coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k: 0.70\n      kendall_tau_cross_family: 0.82\n      # IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap, ARC)\n      irt_mae_arc: 0.028\n      linear_reg_mae_arc: 0.048\n      bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear: 0.032\n      bootstrap_n: 5000\n      # Calibration gating\n      benchmarks_passing_gating: 2\n      gating_pass_arc: true\n      gating_pass_mmlu_stem: true\n      gating_fail_gsm8k: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Cross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment. It\n      elevates the characterization framework from \"Qwen-specific observation\" to\n      \"general benchmark property.\" Here's why each result is expected:\n\n      (1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower\n      ARC baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture\n      (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the\n      model has less headroom — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for\n      science reasoning, producing more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp).\n      This is the critical mechanism: at equivalent parameter count, models with less\n      task-specific capacity show stronger forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION\n      is preserved regardless of architecture. The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct\n      evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark property.\n\n      (2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small\n      for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for\n      reliable recovery. However, the point estimate's SIGN is negative, directionally\n      consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\n\n      (3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved.\n      Adding Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat\n      ARC at 1.5B+/3B) produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points\n      pull the estimate negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The\n      4PL+γ model (appropriate for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal)\n      produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15] that excludes zero. This is within the best tier\n      target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\n\n      (4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same\n      positive direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science >\n      CS_Eng is preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science\n      borderline; CS_Eng null. The direction match across families (both showing\n      Math > Science >> CS_Eng ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination\n      susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density, a property of the benchmark\n      items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes reflect: (a) different pre-training\n      distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama's pre-training), (b) different\n      SFT dynamics (Llama's architecture processes paraphrases differently), but (c)\n      the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by item characteristics.\n\n      (5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format\n      resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven:\n      chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates.\n      This holds across model families.\n\n      (6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer\n      similarity is model-agnostic, confirming S1's robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal\n      replicates (Cohen's d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model\n      memorization dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction,\n      a phenomenon shared across architectures at comparable scale.\n\n      (7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and\n      the 4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The\n      bootstrap test is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship\n      between ability, contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression\n      misses — specifically, the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and\n      ceiling effects in MCQ that produce heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the\n      IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically distinguishable.\n\n      (8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE <\n      raw MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant\n      across both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2\n      benchmarks (of 3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [Domain, \"Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\", \"Llama γ̂ (N=6)\", Direction Match, Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [STEM_Math, \"+0.97 [0.53,1.43]\", \"+0.73 [0.08,1.42]\", \"✓ both positive\", \"Factual recall — both susceptible\"]\n          - [STEM_Science, \"+0.84 [0.39,1.27]\", \"+0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\", \"✓ both positive*\", \"Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\"]\n          - [STEM_CS_Eng, \"+0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\", \"+0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\", \"✓ both null\", \"Procedural — resistant\"]\n          - [Medical, \"−0.20 PPP=1.0\", \"−0.15 PPP=1.0\", \"✓ both misfit\", \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"]\n          - [Humanities, \"−0.41 PPP=1.0\", \"−0.30 PPP=1.0\", \"✓ both misfit\", \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"]\n        note: \"* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.\"\n      - name: \"Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\", \"Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\", \"Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\", \"Direction\"]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"+0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\", \"−1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\", \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\", \"Forgetting (benchmark)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\", \"+0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\", \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\", \"Null (format)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\", \"+0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\", \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\", \"Domain-dependent\"]\n        note: \"* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary specification for MCQ.\"\n      - name: \"Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)\"\n        columns: [Method, MAE, \"95% Bootstrap CI\", \"p vs Linear\"]\n        rows:\n          - [Raw (no adjustment), 0.055, \"[0.038, 0.072]\", \"—\"]\n          - [B4 Linear Regression, 0.048, \"[0.032, 0.064]\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"ContamCal 4PL+γ\", 0.028, \"[0.015, 0.041]\", \"p=0.032\"]\n          - [B3 Vanilla 4PL, 0.062, \"[0.043, 0.081]\", \"p=0.92\"]\n          - [B6 DCR Fuzzy, 0.185, \"[0.140, 0.230]\", \"p<0.001\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: Paired forest plot — domain-level γ for Qwen vs Llama, showing\n         directional consistency. STEM domains (Math, Science) positive; non-STEM PPP=1.0\n         boundary. Error bars overlap but signs match.\"\n      - \"Figure 6: Cross-family ARC dose-response — Llama shows clearer negative slope\n         than Qwen at 3B, converging evidence for ARC forgetting as benchmark property.\"\n      - \"Figure S4: MMLU STEM gradient visualization (γ vs domain) for both families\n         on same axes, showing parallel gradients at different magnitudes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC γ sign\n      tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because\n      Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting\n      (-4.7pp), pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification\n      (appropriate for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30\n      cross-family, ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\n\n      The STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the\n      characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level\n      properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture,\n      tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same\n      benchmark-dependent effects = benchmark×domain property established.\n\n      IRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30\n      provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling\n      effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern #21.\n\n  - name: rl_observational_case_study\n    plan_id: exp-009\n    description: >\n      Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n      (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation,\n      IRT fitting with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and\n      near-zero γ specificity check on known-clean benchmark.\n    expected_results:\n      # Signal quality (RL models)\n      instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.58\n      instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc: 0.55\n      instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.61\n      instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc: 0.60\n      instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.63\n      instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.65\n      deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.57\n      deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.62\n      # S4 shows mild RL-sensitivity (per exp-008 finding)\n      instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu: 0.078\n      instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc: 0.031\n      # IRT results (observational, wider CIs)\n      # Pooled observational + controlled (N≈35-40)\n      obs_arc_gamma: -0.85\n      obs_arc_gamma_ci: [-2.1, 0.4]\n      obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma: 0.18\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.35, 0.72]\n      obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      obs_mmlu_gamma: 0.42\n      obs_mmlu_gamma_ci: [-0.25, 1.10]\n      # Model-specific γ estimates not meaningful (observational confounds)\n      # Expected regret quantification (top-5 model selection)\n      regret_uncalibrated_pp: 3.4\n      regret_calibrated_pp: 1.9\n      regret_remove_rescore_pp: 2.8\n      regret_reduction_pct: 44.1\n      regret_n_candidates: 12\n      regret_top_k: 5\n      regret_ground_truth: \"held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\"\n      # Near-zero γ specificity check\n      clean_benchmark_name: \"WinoGrande\"\n      clean_gamma: 0.04\n      clean_gamma_ci: [-0.55, 0.62]\n      clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero: true\n      specificity_confirmed: true\n      # Coverage (observational)\n      coverage_95_obs: 0.53\n      coverage_95_controlled_only: 0.67\n      # DeepSeek-R1 specific\n      deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b: 0.863\n      deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b: 0.924\n      deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b: 0.542\n      deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b: 0.718\n    result_rationale: >\n      Observational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and weaker γ estimates.\n      The key contributions are directional consistency, practical utility, and\n      specificity validation.\n\n      (1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to\n      0.55-0.65 (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don't know\n      which items these models saw during training, so S1's reference database\n      (original benchmark items) may not match the actual contamination source,\n      (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO) modifies representations beyond what SFT\n      alone does, reducing embedding similarity between model outputs and the\n      benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models are further from base\n      than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores noisier.\n      S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08\n      on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\n\n      (2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with\n      controlled results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure\n      scores are noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal\n      quality is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive\n      direction, consistent with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than\n      controlled experiments — this is honest and expected.\n\n      (3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces\n      expected regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT\n      calibration correctly adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores\n      (likely contaminated) relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs,\n      the DIRECTION of adjustment is correct often enough to improve selection.\n      Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated\n      scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12). The 44% reduction is meaningful\n      for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models with calibrated vs uncalibrated\n      ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\n\n      (4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark\n      is not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less\n      likely to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn't\n      hallucinate contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination\n      is absent. This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any\n      model variation, not specifically contamination.\n\n      (5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924)\n      potentially reflecting distillation from R1's chain-of-thought, which may\n      encode benchmark-specific patterns. However, without controlled injection, we\n      cannot distinguish genuine capability from contamination. The IRT framework\n      assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting this uncertainty.\n\n      (6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67)\n      because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn't\n      fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational\n      extension.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)\"\n        columns: [Strategy, \"Expected Regret (pp)\", \"vs Uncalibrated\", Description]\n        rows:\n          - [Uncalibrated (raw ranking), 3.4, \"—\", \"Rank by raw benchmark scores\"]\n          - [Remove-and-rescore, 2.8, \"−17.6%\", \"Drop flagged items, re-rank\"]\n          - [ContamCal (calibrated), 1.9, \"−44.1%\", \"IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\"]\n          - [Oracle (ground truth), 0.0, \"−100%\", \"Perfect clean scores\"]\n      - name: \"Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\", \"w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\", Direction Consistent]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\", \"−0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\", \"✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\", \"+0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\", \"✓ (null preserved)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\", \"+0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\", \"✓ (positive preserved)\"]\n      - name: \"Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"γ̂\", \"95% CI\", \"Includes 0?\", Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [WinoGrande, 0.04, \"[−0.55, 0.62]\", \"Yes\", \"No false contamination detection\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Expected regret curve — regret vs number of selected models (top-k)\n         for 3 strategies, showing consistent calibration advantage across k=3,5,7,10.\"\n      - \"Figure S5: Observational γ forest plot overlaid on controlled γ estimates.\n         Direction consistent, CIs wider. Gray band = controlled 95% CI.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1) directional\n      consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond controlled\n      SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even with noisy\n      signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null results on\n      clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual γ estimates\n      are honest — we frame this as \"directional external validity\" not \"quantitative\n      confirmation.\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination\n      interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data\n      is a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and\n      observational designs.\n\n  - name: observational_72b\n    plan_id: exp-012\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals and\n      benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled\n      IRT. No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\n    expected_results:\n      # Eval accuracies (72B base or Instruct, inference-only)\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.848\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.916\n      arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.944\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.628\n      # Signal scores (72B)\n      s1_exposure_mmlu: 0.11\n      s1_exposure_arc: 0.07\n      s1_exposure_gsm8k: 0.14\n      s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean: 1.69\n      s2_minkpp_arc_mean: 1.68\n      s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy: 2.35\n      # Pooled IRT with 72B (adds 1 data point to N≈30 cross-family pool)\n      pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma: -1.15\n      pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci: [-2.20, -0.12]\n      pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma: 0.07\n      pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci: [-0.21, 0.36]\n      pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma: 0.50\n      pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci: [-0.16, 1.18]\n      pooled_with_72b_n: 31\n      # 72B individual calibration deltas\n      arc_72b_raw: 0.944\n      arc_72b_calibrated: 0.948\n      arc_72b_delta: +0.004\n      mmlu_72b_raw: 0.848\n      mmlu_72b_calibrated: 0.843\n      mmlu_72b_delta: -0.005\n      gsm8k_72b_raw: 0.916\n      gsm8k_72b_calibrated: 0.916\n      gsm8k_72b_delta: 0.000\n      # Coverage and ranking improvement\n      coverage_95_with_72b: 0.68\n      kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.84\n      # GPU cost\n      inference_gpu_hours: 12\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination\n      effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\n\n      (1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K.\n      These are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance\n      is ~89.8% MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination\n      inflation is small relative to genuine capability.\n\n      (2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range\n      in controlled SFT models). The 72B model's training data likely includes some\n      benchmark-adjacent material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1\n      similarity. But these scores are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models,\n      so the IRT model correctly treats 72B as a low-exposure data point.\n\n      (3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from\n      the forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \"un-forgets\" for 72B, consistent\n      with the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true\n      ability than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent\n      with positive MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\n\n      (4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability\n      distribution (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point\n      helps anchor item difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation\n      precision for all models. This is analogous to including very high-ability\n      examinees in educational testing — they provide information about difficult\n      items that lower-ability examinees cannot. Kendall's τ improves from 0.82 to\n      0.84 due to better separation at the high end of the ability distribution.\n\n      (5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30\n      has minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but\n      ecological validity at frontier scale.\n\n      (6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus\n      computing 3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires\n      full forward passes on ~16K items.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, Raw Accuracy, Calibrated, \"Δ (pp)\", Interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.848, 0.843, \"−0.5\", \"Mild STEM inflation correction\"]\n          - [ARC, 0.944, 0.948, \"+0.4\", \"Forgetting model slight upward correction\"]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.916, 0.916, \"0.0\", \"Null γ → no adjustment\"]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.628, 0.628, \"0.0\", \"Not calibrated (appendix-only)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S6: Calibrated-vs-raw accuracy scatter across all scales (0.5B\n         to 72B). 72B cluster on the diagonal — minimal correction. Dashed line\n         = identity. Lower scales show larger deviations.\"\n      - \"Inset in Figure 3: 72B point added to dose-response plot at d=unknown,\n         positioned at observed accuracy with wide CI bar.\"\n    analysis: >\n      72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination\n      effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are\n      within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\"the framework\n      applies from 0.5B to 72B\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation.\n      Paper frames as: \"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable\n      estimates of true ability — ContamCal's value concentrates at smaller scales\n      where contamination effects are measurable.\"\n\n  - name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n    plan_id: exp-013\n    description: >\n      Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance gap\n      between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse).\n      No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation\n      when only API-level signals are available.\n    expected_results:\n      # 2-signal RRF quality\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.52\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc: 0.53\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k: 0.51\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.52\n      rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.68\n      rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean: 0.98\n      # IRT with 2-signal exposure (2PL+γ, pooled N=30)\n      arc_gamma_2signal: -0.48\n      arc_gamma_2signal_ci: [-2.4, 1.5]\n      arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal: 0.06\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci: [-1.0, 1.1]\n      gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      mmlu_gamma_2signal: 0.28\n      mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci: [-1.2, 1.8]\n      # Calibration metrics\n      arc_mae_2signal: 0.049\n      arc_mae_4signal_s1only: 0.028\n      arc_mae_raw: 0.055\n      gsm8k_mae_2signal: 0.012\n      gsm8k_mae_raw: 0.006\n      mmlu_mae_2signal: 0.014\n      mmlu_mae_raw: 0.014\n      # Coverage comparison\n      coverage_95_2signal_arc: 0.40\n      coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k: 0.47\n      coverage_95_4signal_arc: 0.67\n      coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k: 0.70\n      # Ranking\n      kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n      kendall_tau_4signal: 0.82\n      # Calibration gating\n      benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal: 0\n      gating_fail_reason_arc: \"γ CI includes zero\"\n      gating_fail_reason_gsm8k: \"γ CI includes zero (expected)\"\n      gating_fail_reason_mmlu: \"γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\"\n      # RRF decomposition\n      s3_constat_d005_auc_mean: 0.53\n      s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean: 0.50\n      s3_contribution_to_rrf: 0.55\n      s4_contribution_to_rrf: 0.45\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding similarity)\n      carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\n\n      (1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between\n      original and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated,\n      so the performance gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing\n      paraphrases for evaluation — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B,\n      position bias (D045) confounds this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat\n      better but still limited by the small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from\n      exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\n\n      (2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse\n      requires the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive\n      capability that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows\n      mild improvement per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this\n      is insufficient for reliable discrimination.\n\n      (3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two\n      near-chance signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the\n      fusion preserves the best individual signal's ranking, which is itself\n      near-random.\n\n      (4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially\n      noise. Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48,\n      still negative) because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to\n      partially identify γ even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive\n      and all include zero. (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to\n      0.049, approaching raw MAE (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012\n      (raw 0.006) — calibration with noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage\n      collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67 (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores\n      produce poorly calibrated posteriors with wrong widths. (d) Kendall's τ drops\n      to 0.55 — barely better than random ranking.\n\n      (5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero).\n      The gating criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a\n      FEATURE, not a failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the\n      gating criterion's necessity and ContamCal's honest self-assessment.\n\n      (6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently\n      viable with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the\n      load-bearing component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible\n      detection methods (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination\n      detection, or model providers exposing limited embedding access).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality\"\n        columns: [Mode, Signals, \"Mean AUC\", \"ARC MAE\", \"ARC Cov95\", \"Kendall τ\", \"Gating\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"S1-only (primary)\", \"S1\", 0.98, 0.028, 0.67, 0.82, \"2/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"4-signal RRF\", \"S1+S2+S3+S4\", 0.68, 0.030, 0.67, 0.82, \"2/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"2-signal (closed)\", \"S3+S4\", 0.52, 0.049, 0.40, 0.55, \"0/3 pass\"]\n          - [\"No calibration\", \"—\", \"—\", 0.055, \"—\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n        note: >\n          4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information.\n          2-signal mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents\n          2-signal calibration from producing misleading results.\n      - name: \"Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"γ̂\", \"CI excl 0?\", \"PPP\", \"cal<raw?\", Gating]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, \"−0.48\", \"No\", 0.35, \"Yes (0.049<0.055)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig)\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"+0.06\", \"No\", 0.48, \"No (0.012>0.006)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\"]\n          - [MMLU, \"+0.28\", \"No\", 0.30, \"No (0.014≈0.014)\", \"FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S7: Three-panel bar chart comparing MAE, Coverage-95, and Kendall-τ\n         across signal modes (S1-only, 4-signal, 2-signal, raw). Clear monotonic\n         degradation from S1-only to 2-signal.\"\n      - \"Figure S8: Exposure score distributions for 2-signal vs 4-signal RRF,\n         showing 2-signal scores compressed near uniform (no discrimination) vs\n         4-signal bimodal separation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates:\n      (1) ContamCal's calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality —\n      not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses\n      via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary\n      for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight\n      gap motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes\n      that at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008:\n      delta≈0.08 MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable\n      for closed-weight models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"89efc4bf-b4a8-4592-8628-12957d10d789","total_cost_usd":1.2183374000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21609,"cache_read_input_tokens":175796,"output_tokens":34602,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21609,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":21199,"cache_read_input_tokens":68688,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6475,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6475},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":38466,"outputTokens":3515,"cacheReadInputTokens":155709,"cacheCreationInputTokens":46961,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.13031315000000002,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":34602,"cacheReadInputTokens":175796,"cacheCreationInputTokens":21609,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.0880242500000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"114194fa-4c50-4cc9-b393-7916466139fb"}
